\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Covariance and variance of Gaussian observables}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{\mu_Q} be the [Gaussian law](fdip-0005) on a finite real
inner-product space #{(E,h)}, and write #{\omega_Q} for its
[expectation](fdip-0006). For Borel #{X,Y:E\to\mathbb R} with
#{X}, #{Y} and #{XY} absolutely integrable, define their
\newvocab{covariance} by
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}_Q(X,Y)
 &=\omega_Q(XY)-\omega_Q(X)\omega_Q(Y)\\
 &=\omega_Q\bigl((X-\omega_Q(X))(Y-\omega_Q(Y))\bigr).
\end{aligned}}
Expansion and normalization prove the second equality; all four terms
are integrable by the stated hypotheses. This extends the finite-sum
\vocabk{covariance}{ftip-008H} to integrals.}
\p{When #{X} and #{X^2} are integrable, its \newvocab{variance} is
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}_Q(X)&=\operatorname{Cov}_Q(X,X)\\
 &=\omega_Q\bigl((X-\omega_Q(X))^2\bigr)\\
 &=\omega_Q(X^2)-\omega_Q(X)^2\geq0.
\end{aligned}}
The inequality is positivity of the integral. In particular all these
expressions exist for polynomials by \ref{fdip-002W}, consistently with
the [polynomial state](fdip-002X).}
\p{For #{\ell_j(x)=h(j,x)}, set #{K(j,k)=\operatorname{Cov}_Q(\ell_j,\ell_k)}.
It is a finite symmetric bilinear form. There is a unique linear
\newvocab{covariance operator} #{C_Q:E\to E}, relative to the specified
#{h}, such that #{h(j,C_Qk)=K(j,k)}.}
\proof{
\p{In an orthonormal basis put #{C_Qk=\sum_i K(u_i,k)u_i}.
Bilinearity of #{K} follows from integral linearity and gives the
linearity of #{C_Q}. Orthonormal expansion proves the required pairing.
If #{C_Q'} has the same pairings, then
#{h(j,(C_Q-C_Q')k)=0} for every #{j,k}; choosing
#{j=(C_Q-C_Q')k} proves equality. Thus the construction is independent
of the basis. At #{E=0} it is the unique map. This representation
does not identify #{C_Q} with inverse precision.}
}
\p{For the counting field space, the coordinate means vanish by
\ref{fdip-002Y}, and the former coordinate notation is
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Cov}_Q(u,v)
 &=\left\langle(\phi_u-\langle\phi_u\rangle_Q)
                    (\phi_v-\langle\phi_v\rangle_Q)\right\rangle_Q\\
 &=\langle\phi_u\phi_v\rangle_Q.
\end{aligned}}
The partition function, this two-point expectation, and the expectation
of a different observable are different quantities. The separate
[inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B) computes this covariance.}
\p{The definitions extend \ref{ftip-008H} directly; the integral identities
use the preceding moment and state results. Positivity uses the ordinary
real convention of \citet{Definition 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}
through \ref{fdip-0024}, not an additional inverse-covariance assumption.}
